# November 22, 2026: a second scatter of places across North Carolina, which had 30 (the first scatter of nc_qvkt, Old
# Salem and Winston-Salem, Brunswick Town and the Capitol). From Corolla and Hertford in the northeast to Asheville and
# Blowing Rock in the mountains: the Roanoke Canal, the Newbold-White House, Fort Branch, Bentonville, Tryon Palace, the
# Currituck Beach Light, Wilmington's Bellamy Mansion, Rocky Mount Mills, Hillsborough's old courthouse, the House in
# the Horseshoe, the Spencer Shops, Fort Dobbs, Murray's Mill and the Bunker Hill Covered Bridge, Morganton's old
# courthouse, Flat Top Manor and the Thomas Wolfe house.
# Facts come from the linked sources only, read in this session: Wikipedia through tools/wiki_raw.py, HMdb markers, and
# the managing bodies' own pages (NC Historic Sites, Tryon Palace, the NC Transportation Museum, the National Park
# Service, the Historical Association of Catawba County, the Roanoke Canal Commission, the Perquimans County Restoration
# Association, the Fort Branch Historical Society, the Bellamy Mansion Museum) through tools/page.py. Hours are as those
# pages gave them on November 22, 2026.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'nc_iodc','label':'North Carolina: a second scatter, Corolla to Asheville','added':'2026-11-22 08:30'}
NCHS='https://historicsites.nc.gov/all-sites/'
STATEHRS='Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Sunday, Monday and state holidays'
