PLACES=[
N('roanoke-canal-trail','Roanoke Canal Museum and Trail','Roanoke Rapids','nc','canal','traces',36.47474,-77.64819,'site','open','walk','canal','1821–1823 (aqueduct)',
  'Part of the Roanoke Navigation, planned to link the Blue Ridge with the Atlantic, the canal runs about seven miles from Roanoke Rapids Lake down to its return into the Roanoke River at Weldon. Its first sections were built under the engineer Hamilton Fulton. It was listed on the National Register in 1976. Later the water drove a brick mill of 1892 and a power house of about 1904.',
  'Stretches of the 39-foot channel and its 10-foot towpath, the original aqueduct of 1821 to 1823 and a stone culvert survive, and the towpath and canal bed now make a walking and cycling trail between Roanoke Rapids and Weldon. Wikipedia also lists the middle or lift locks among the surviving works. An HMdb marker in Weldon points to the aqueduct over Chockoyotte Creek about three quarters of a mile west, towards Ponderosa Road. The pin is the museum at the Roanoke Rapids end of the trail.',
  'Seven miles of 1820s canal you can walk, aqueduct and all.',
  'The trail is overseen by the Roanoke Canal Commission and is open from dawn to dusk every day. The museum is at 15 Jackson Street Extension, Roanoke Rapids; admission $4 for anyone 9 and over (less for Halifax County residents), free for 8 and under. Hours from the museum\'s own page.',
  [W('Roanoke Canal'),L('Roanoke Canal Museum: hours and tickets','https://roanokecanal.com/107-roanoke-canal-museum-and-trail-general-information/299-welcome-to-the-roanoke-canal-museum-and-trail.html'),L('Roanoke Canal Museum: the trail','https://roanokecanal.com/roanoke-canal-trail.html'),L('HMdb: Roanoke Canal marker list near Weldon','https://www.hmdb.org/nearbylist.asp?nearby=yes&Latitude=36.43&Longitude=-77.62&Miles=5&MilesType=1')],
  brick='?',hours='Museum Tuesday, Thursday and Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; trail dawn to dusk daily'),
N('newbold-white-house','Newbold-White House','Hertford','nc','landmark','standing',36.16601,-76.44159,'site','open','drive','','1730',
  'A house on the Perquimans River that tells the story of Abraham and Judith Sanders, a colonial Quaker family. It dates to 1730, is listed on the National Register, and its keepers call it the oldest brick house in North Carolina.',
  'The house has been restored, with enormous fireplaces, pine woodwork and a winding corner stair, and period furnishings inside. Guided tours go inside on Saturdays in season; the grounds can be walked on a self-guided tour at any time.',
  'The oldest brick house in North Carolina, by its keepers\' account.',
  'Run by the Perquimans County Restoration Association, 151 Newbold White Road, Hertford. Guided tours on Saturdays from early May to late October, or by appointment at other times with two weeks\' notice; self-guided tours of the grounds are always welcome (the association\'s page).',
  [L('Perquimans County Restoration Association: Newbold-White House','https://www.perquimansrestoration.org/newbold-white-house'),W('Newbold–White House')],
  brick='yes',hours='Guided tours Saturdays 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., May to October; other times by appointment'),
N('fort-branch-hamilton','Fort Branch','Hamilton','nc','fort','traces',35.92826,-77.17224,'area','open','walk','forts','1862–1865',
  'A Confederate earthen fort built in 1862 on Rainbow Banks, about 70 feet above a bend of the Roanoke River southeast of Hamilton. Its guns guarded the river below the Wilmington and Weldon Railroad bridge at Weldon and the yard upriver where the ironclad C.S.S. Albemarle was being built. A Union column advanced on it in December 1864.',
  'The earthworks with rebuilt gun emplacements, and eight of the fort\'s original eleven cannons, which the historical society says makes it the only earthen fort in the South with its own artillery in place. There is also a restored late-1800s steam engine from the boat Ranger, local Native American pottery from Hoggtown, and an 1850s farmhouse under restoration.',
  'A river fort that kept its own cannons.',
  'Kept by the Fort Branch Historical Society, 2883 Fort Branch Road, Oak City (mailing address Hamilton). A battle re-enactment is held on the first weekend in November and a candlelight Christmas tour on the first Saturday in December at 7 p.m. Regular opening days and times were not on the society\'s home page and its tour page could not be opened when this was written.',
  [W('Fort Branch'),L('Fort Branch Civil War Site','https://fortbranchcivilwarsite.com')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('bentonville-battlefield','Bentonville Battlefield and the Harper House','Four Oaks','nc','fort','traces',35.30222,-78.32237,'area','open','drive','','1865',
  'The battle of Bentonville, March 19 to 21, 1865, was the largest fought in North Carolina and the last full-scale action of the Civil War in which a Confederate army mounted a tactical offensive, against General Sherman\'s army marching through the Carolinas. The state owns about a third of the field, a National Historic Landmark since 1996.',
  'The Harper House, the only building left from the time of the battle, used as a field hospital by the Union\'s Fourteenth Corps; it is restored and toured, with a reconstructed kitchen and quarters for the enslaved. Outside are the Union Twentieth Corps reserve trenches, the Harper family cemetery, a Confederate cemetery, monuments, nearly 5 miles of walking trails along original trenches and a 10-mile driving tour with eight stops.',
  'The trenches of North Carolina\'s largest battle.',
  'Bentonville Battlefield State Historic Site (NC Historic Sites), visitor center at 5466 Harper House Road, Four Oaks. Admission free; guided tours have a fee. The pin is the visitor center; the Harper House is beside it, as Wikipedia says.',
  [W('Bentonville Battlefield'),L('NC Historic Sites: Bentonville Battlefield',NCHS+'bentonville-battlefield')],
  brick='?',hours=STATEHRS),
N('tryon-palace','Tryon Palace','New Bern','nc','landmark','standing',35.10581,-77.04431,'site','open','walk','','1770; rebuilt 1950s',
  'The governor\'s house designed by John Hawks and finished in 1770, the seat of the royal governors until provincial militia seized it in 1775, and the state house until the capital moved to Raleigh in 1792. It burned in 1798. The palace standing now is a reconstruction on the original foundations, built from the original plans in the 1950s and opened as a museum in 1959.',
  'The Stable Office is the one original building still standing; the palace and its kitchen office are rebuilt. The grounds have 18th-century style gardens, and the North Carolina History Center, opened in 2010, stands near by. The palace is seen on guided tours.',
  'A royal governor\'s palace, rebuilt from its own plans; only the stables are original.',
  'A state-owned historic site, Tryon Palace. Palace tours Monday to Friday and Saturday 10:30 a.m. to 2:30 p.m., Sunday 12:30 to 2:30 p.m.; the Kitchen Office is self-touring. Closed Thanksgiving, December 24 to 26 and New Year\'s Day. Hours from Tryon Palace\'s own page; tickets on its tickets page. Parking off Eden Street.',
  [W('Tryon Palace'),L('Tryon Palace: hours of operation','https://www.tryonpalace.org/hours-operation'),L('Tryon Palace: tickets and tours','https://www.tryonpalace.org/plan-your-visit/tickets')],
  brick='yes',hours='NC History Center Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., Sunday noon to 5; palace tours 10:30 to 2:30 (Sunday from 12:30)',th=[35.10592,-77.04541],thn='Lot off Eden Street'),
N('currituck-beach-light','Currituck Beach Light','Corolla','nc','light','standing',36.37667,-75.83066,'exact','open','walk','','1875',
  'Finished on December 1, 1875 between Cape Henry and Bodie Island, the last major brick lighthouse built on the Outer Banks. It was automated in 1939. The Outer Banks Conservationists, who own it, have restored it since 1980.',
  'Unlike its neighbours it was never painted, so its red brick shows. Visitors have been allowed to climb the original 220 steps to the outdoor gallery since 1991; the lens room is shut, because the first-order lens is the original and still works.',
  'The Outer Banks lighthouse left in bare brick.',
  'Owned by the Outer Banks Conservationists, on Corolla Village Road. The climbing season, hours and fee were not found on a page of the owner\'s that could be read when this was written.',
  [W('Currituck Beach Light'),W('Cape Lookout Lighthouse')],
  brick='yes',hours='?'),
N('bellamy-mansion','Bellamy Mansion','Wilmington','nc','landmark','standing',34.2361,-77.9429,'exact','open','walk','','1859–1861',
  'A 22-room house in Greek Revival and Italianate styles built for Dr. John D. Bellamy between 1859 and 1861 by enslaved and free Black craftsmen, to the design of James F. Post. Federal troops made it their headquarters after Wilmington fell in 1865. Arsonists set it on fire in 1972; it was restored over two decades and is now a museum, a stewardship property of Preservation North Carolina.',
  'The house and, behind it, the slave quarters and a small carriage house, both of red brick. The restored urban slave quarters are among the best in the state and one of very few open to the public. Self-guided audio tours (a smartphone is needed) and guided tours go inside.',
  'A city mansion with its brick slave quarters still behind it.',
  'Bellamy Mansion Museum, 503 Market Street, Wilmington, with a free parking lot on Market Street. Admission charged; premium guided tours at 10 a.m., noon and 2 p.m. when available. Hours from the museum\'s own page.',
  [W('Bellamy Mansion'),L('Bellamy Mansion Museum','https://www.bellamymansion.org/')],
  brick='some',hours='Daily 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. (self-guided tours start by 4 and end by 5)'),
N('rocky-mount-mills','Rocky Mount Mills','Rocky Mount','nc','industry','standing',35.95957,-77.80139,'site','public','walk','','1816',
  'The second cotton mill built in North Carolina, founded on the Tar River in 1816 by Joel Battle and Henry A. Donaldson. From 1817 its workforce was entirely Black, most of them enslaved, for thirty-four years, the first use of slave labour in a cotton mill in the state. Capitol Broadcasting bought the 150-acre mill property in 2007.',
  'The mill has been turned into lofts, offices and event space, and the campus round it holds craft breweries and restaurants, so the old buildings can be walked round and the businesses entered in their opening hours. Which mill buildings date from which year was not checked.',
  'North Carolina\'s second cotton mill, now a brewery village.',
  'Privately owned: Capitol Broadcasting Company bought the property in 2007 (Wikipedia) and runs it as a campus of breweries, restaurants, offices and lofts at 1151 Falls Road, Rocky Mount. The public comes and goes through its businesses; business hours not checked.',
  [W('Rocky Mount Mills')],
  brick='?'),
N('old-orange-county-courthouse','Old Orange County Courthouse','Hillsborough','nc','civic','standing',36.07501,-79.09907,'exact','view','walk','','1845',
  'A two-storey Greek Revival courthouse of 1845, temple-form, which replaced Dickerson Chapel as the county courthouse. It was the seat of Orange County\'s government until a new building was finished in 1954. It was listed on the National Register in 1971 and stands in the Hillsborough Historic District.',
  'A brick building laid in Flemish bond, with a pedimented Doric portico of four columns and a two-stage clock tower. Interior not checked.',
  'A temple-front courthouse with a clock tower, in one of the state\'s oldest towns.',
  'At 106 East King Street, Hillsborough, in the historic district; seen from the street. Whether and when the inside can be visited was not checked.',
  [W('Old Orange County Courthouse (North Carolina)')],
  brick='yes'),
N('house-in-the-horseshoe','House in the Horseshoe (Alston House)','Sanford','nc','landmark','standing',35.46711,-79.38347,'exact','open','drive','','1772',
  'Philip Alston built the house in 1772 in a horseshoe bend of the Deep River. In 1781 loyalists under David Fanning attacked it and Alston\'s patriot militia; the fight ended in Alston\'s surrender, with his wife negotiating the terms. In 1798 it was sold to Benjamin Williams, later governor, who enslaved about 103 people and grew cotton there.',
  'The 1772 house, now a museum and the scene of a Revolutionary War re-enactment and living-history days each year. Interior not checked.',
  'A 1772 house that was the scene of a fight between neighbours in the Revolution.',
  'House in the Horseshoe State Historic Site (NC Historic Sites), Alston House Road, Moore County, with a lot beside the house. Closed until further notice while a new visitor center is built: the state said in November 2025 that the work would run to early 2027. Check the site\'s page before going.',
  [W('House in the Horseshoe'),L('NC Historic Sites: House in the Horseshoe',NCHS+'house-horseshoe'),L('NC DNCR: construction notice, November 2025','https://www.dncr.nc.gov/news/press-releases/2025/11/17/house-horseshoe-state-historic-site-announces-construction-will-begin-new-visitor-center-december')],
  brick='?',hours='Closed while a new visitor center is built; reopening planned for early 2027',th=[35.46660,-79.38343],thn='Lot on Alston House Road'),
N('spencer-shops','Spencer Shops (North Carolina Transportation Museum)','Spencer','nc','rail','standing',35.68694,-80.43611,'site','open','walk','stations','1890s–1970s',
  'The Southern Railway\'s main locomotive repair shops, built in the 1890s halfway along its line from Washington to Atlanta and named after its president, Samuel Spencer. The big buildings were steel frames on heavy masonry. After diesels came the work dwindled and the shops closed in the 1970s; in 1977 the railway gave the back shop and other buildings to the state, which made them the North Carolina Transportation Museum.',
  'The 37-stall roundhouse of 1924 and its 100-foot electric turntable still stand, with the back shop, the master mechanic\'s office, a warehouse and the flue shop. The blacksmith, boiler and woodworking shops were pulled down by 1965. Seasonal train rides run on the site.',
  'A steam-age roundhouse and turntable, still turning.',
  'North Carolina Transportation Museum, a state site in Spencer. Admission and train-ride tickets are sold on its page; hours change through the year (shorter around the December holiday train event). Hours from the museum\'s own page.',
  [W('Southern Railway Spencer Shops'),L('North Carolina Transportation Museum','https://www.nctrans.org')],
  brick='?',hours='March to Labor Day Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., Sunday noon to 5; January and February Tuesday to Saturday 9 to 5'),
N('fort-dobbs','Fort Dobbs','Statesville','nc','fort','ruins',35.82217,-80.89713,'site','open','walk','forts','1756–1761',
  'A frontier fort of the French and Indian War, named after Governor Arthur Dobbs and garrisoned from 1756 to 1761: a log blockhouse with a shallow ditch round it and, by 1759, a palisade. Digging from 1967 confirmed the site, finding the moat and the post holes of the log building. It is the only state historic site of that war.',
  'Trails lead through the excavated remains of the fort, and beside them stands a full reconstruction of the blockhouse, built from 2016 and finished in September 2019. The Daughters of the American Revolution put up a stone marker on the site by 1910.',
  'A French and Indian War blockhouse, dug up and built again beside its own post holes.',
  'Fort Dobbs State Historic Site (NC Historic Sites), 438 Fort Dobbs Road, Statesville. Grounds and visitor center free; guided tours $5 adults, $4 seniors and military, $1 youth (as of August 2025). Check the site\'s Facebook page for closings.',
  [W('Fort Dobbs (North Carolina)'),L('NC Historic Sites: Fort Dobbs',NCHS+'fort-dobbs')],
  brick='no',hours=STATEHRS),
N('murrays-mill','Murray\'s Mill','Catawba','nc','industry','standing',35.67619,-81.09392,'site','open','walk','','1913 mill (site from 1873)',
  'A working roller mill on Balls Creek, run by three generations of the Murray family until 1967, the last milling complex in Catawba County. The Catawba County Historical Association began restoring it in 1980 and opened it in 1988.',
  'The two-storey frame mill of 1913 with its 28-foot waterwheel still turning below the pond and dam, the Murray and Minges General Store of the 1890s, the wheathouse of the 1880s (now an exhibit gallery), the John Murray House of 1913 furnished to the period, and outbuildings. Over 3 miles of trails go round the pond, woods, fields and creek. Guided and self-guided tours.',
  'A country roller mill with its 28-foot wheel still going round.',
  'Historical Association of Catawba County, 1489 Murray\'s Mill Road, Catawba. Grounds and trails open in daylight; leashed dogs welcome; no fishing or kayaking. Guided tours $10, self-guided $7, military and veterans free. Hours from the association\'s page.',
  [W('Murray\'s Mill Historic District'),L('Historical Association of Catawba County: Murray\'s Mill','https://www.catawbahistory.org/locations/murrays-mill-historic-district'),L('Historical Association of Catawba County: hours',CATAWBA)],
  brick='no',hours='Wednesday to Friday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., Saturday 9 to 5, Sunday 1 to 5'),
N('bunker-hill-covered-bridge','Bunker Hill Covered Bridge','Claremont','nc','landmark','standing',35.72152,-81.11523,'exact','public','walk','','1895',
  'One of two covered bridges left in North Carolina, built in 1895 by Andrew Loretz Ramsour over Lyle Creek on the old Island Ford Road, and the only surviving wooden example of Herman Haupt\'s improved lattice truss. It was built as an open span and roofed in 1900; tin replaced the shingles in 1921. The Bolick family gave it to the county historical association in 1985, and it was restored in 1994.',
  'The 91-foot bridge with its Haupt trusses, a National Historic Civil Engineering Landmark since 2001. It is reached on foot from the park lot.',
  'The last wooden Haupt truss bridge in the country.',
  'Historical Association of Catawba County, off Highway 70 near Claremont. Free; open dawn to dusk daily. Park in the lot on Covered Bridge Park Road and walk to the bridge.',
  [W('Bunker Hill Covered Bridge'),L('Historical Association of Catawba County: Bunker Hill Covered Bridge','https://www.catawbahistory.org/locations/bunker-hill-covered-bridge')],
  brick='no',hours='Dawn to dusk daily',th=[35.71990,-81.11640],thn='Covered Bridge Park lot'),
N('old-burke-county-courthouse','Old Burke County Courthouse','Morganton','nc','civic','standing',35.74571,-81.68739,'exact','open','walk','','1833–1837',
  'A square two-storey stone courthouse in the Classical Revival style, built in 1833 to 1835 for $15,000 and in use by 1837; the cupola was added in a remodelling of 1901. The state supreme court sat here every August from 1847 to 1862. In April 1865 Union cavalry of Stoneman\'s Raid occupied it and destroyed most of the county\'s records. It was the courthouse until 1976 and reopened as a museum in 1984.',
  'The stone building on its raised basement, with pedimented porticoes on two sides and the 1901 cupola. Inside is a museum of the history of law in Morganton.',
  'A stone courthouse Stoneman\'s raiders spared.',
  'At 102 East Union Street, Morganton, kept by the Historic Burke Foundation. The foundation\'s own page could not be opened when this was written; the county tourism page gives two different sets of weekday hours (Monday to Thursday 9 a.m. to 2 p.m., and Monday 9 to 2 with Tuesday to Friday 9 to 3), so call before going.',
  [W('Burke County Courthouse (North Carolina)'),L('Discover Burke County: Historic Burke County Courthouse','https://www.discoverburkecounty.com/all-attractions/historic-burke-county-courthouse/')],
  brick='no',hours='?',conf='mixed'),
N('flat-top-manor','Flat Top Manor (Moses H. Cone Memorial Park)','Blowing Rock','nc','landmark','standing',36.1491,-81.6929,'exact','open','drive','','1899–1900',
  'The summer house of the textile magnate Moses H. Cone and his wife Bertha, a 23-room mansion of about 13,000 square feet built in 1899 and 1900 on an estate on what is now the Blue Ridge Parkway. The family gave the estate to the National Park Service in 1949.',
  'The manor, its outside restored in 2021, with a Southern Highland Craft Guild shop and a park store inside; the carriage house, the Cone cemetery of 1908, the apple barn, a trout lake, Bass Lake and about 25 miles of carriage trails. Rangers lead tours and open houses of the manor in summer and fall.',
  'A textile king\'s summer house on the Parkway.',
  'Blue Ridge Parkway (National Park Service), Moses H. Cone Memorial Park at milepost 294, with a lot beside the manor. The grounds and carriage trails are open to walk; the manor\'s hours were not given in plain text on the park\'s page.',
  [W('Moses H. Cone Memorial Park'),L('NPS: Moses H. Cone Memorial Park, milepost 294','https://www.nps.gov/blri/planyourvisit/moses-h-cone-memorial-park-mp-294.htm')],
  brick='?',hours='?',th=[36.14952,-81.69231],thn='Manor lot, Blue Ridge Parkway'),
N('thomas-wolfe-memorial','Thomas Wolfe Memorial','Asheville','nc','landmark','standing',35.59767,-82.55088,'exact','open','walk','','1883',
  'A two-storey frame house of 1883 in the Queen Anne manner that was already a boarding house, "Old Kentucky Home", when the writer Thomas Wolfe\'s mother bought it in 1906. Wolfe lived there until he went to university in 1916 and made it "Dixieland" in Look Homeward, Angel. An arsonist\'s fire in 1998 destroyed the dining room and about 200 of the 800 original objects; it reopened in 2003 after restoration.',
  'The rambling boarding house with its furnishings, shown on guided tours. A National Historic Landmark since 1971, in the Downtown Asheville Historic District.',
  'The boarding house of Look Homeward, Angel.',
  'Thomas Wolfe Memorial State Historic Site (NC Historic Sites), 52 North Market Street, Asheville. Admission free; guided tours $8 adults, $7 seniors and military, $1 youth (plus tax).',
  [W('Thomas Wolfe House'),L('NC Historic Sites: Thomas Wolfe Memorial',NCHS+'thomas-wolfe-memorial')],
  brick='no',hours=STATEHRS),
]
